==English==

===Etymology===
{{compound|cup|cake}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkʌpkeɪk/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A small cake baked in a paper container shaped like a cup, often with icing on top.
# {{context|slang|lang=en}} An attractive young woman.
#* '''2006''', Thomas Pynchon, ''Against the Day'', Vintage 2007, p. 604:
#*: Whatever else this '''cupcake''' might be up to, she was no piker.
# {{context|dated|lang=en}} {{non-gloss definition|Used as a term of endearment}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|cupcak|es}}

# {{rfv-sense}} {{cx|transitive|computing|lang=en}} To fail to properly perform a computer-related procedure; or, the failure of a computer hardware or software component.
#* {{quote-magazine
|year=2011
|date=October 13
|magazine=Big Bang Theory
|publisher=Chuck Lorre Productions - Warner Brothers Television
|passage=Sorry I got here so late, two systems cupcaked when I was just about to leave the university.}}
#* {{quote-magazine
|year=2011
|date=Fall 2011
|magazine=Information Week
|publisher=UBM TechWeb
|passage=Trying to flash the firmware on a controller this old can be tricky; all one can hope for is that it does not '''cupcake''' the raid configuration.}}
#* {{quote-magazine
|year=2011
|date=Spring 2011
|magazine=Network World
|publisher=Network World, Inc
|passage=Two of the routers were '''cupcaked''' by the open-source code update, due to an incompatibility with the chipset.}}
#* {{quote-magazine
|year=2012
|date=Spring 2012
|magazine=CFO Weekly
|publisher=CXO Media INC
|passage=With today's rapid hardware development cycles, the ROI on server repairs or upgrades can lengthy depending on the mode of failure. Disposing of or recyling a obsolete or '''cupcaked''' system is often a more attractive alternative, and can have tax benefits to the organization.}}

